#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539115} :activity/id #uuid "54d27f54-889d-5c5d-83de-87ccc0d716e1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050293 :topic/id #uuid "3d717848-5743-5882-aa66-adea2ee38d55" :topic/math-academy-id 1980} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6521128c-8b33-54da-8c83-d27bef687244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435529} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9c4d22-52c4-582f-b256-0ddd6fa135ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1,-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435530} {:answer/id #uuid "84cf53e7-7029-5a30-8dd0-ad54d0eb0064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435533} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd79e5d-b439-5f83-a72f-40d75fd4cdd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,-1,-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435537} {:answer/id #uuid "d89ebc6c-8c25-5ee8-a102-c0421505fca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435540} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1008d1e-ad25-59f1-8249-03f1bd3c30fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e0b70f07-48f3-5e91-bb4d-83ea4566f9c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-129560" :question/problem "At which point on the surface $z=4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector $v=⟨-2,-5,1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2})=-4x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2})=-10y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨-4x,\\,-10y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-2,-5,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨-4x,\\,-10y,\\,-1⟩=⟨-2,-5,1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4cx &=-2 \\\\ -10cy &=-5 \\\\ -c &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-4(-1)x=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n-10(-1)y=-5\\,⟹\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4-2{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-5{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4}).$"} :step/id #uuid "2b5d0276-eb2f-5df6-bfcf-46f02d9b5cdd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539054 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11da6c8b-9969-5900-9b7a-9809072650d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x+2y-3z-12w+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263165} {:answer/id #uuid "65aca46c-892b-57cc-bbda-306a11d68a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y+z-4w+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263880} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fbfa20-0a93-5e20-ad08-85bb7ababd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-z-8w+18=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264558} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd3eeee-85c8-5935-bbbf-97214b8aff26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-y-2z-4w+22=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264650} {:answer/id #uuid "df92e64a-c76e-5206-b481-adf89451351c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x-y-2z+6w-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263165} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0ebee6-3d61-5917-b532-d12fc16fdb57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba77fa6c-6a35-5c61-ab53-8720f1df1a35" :question/math-academy-id "q-129476" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z}$ at the point $(1,9,4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,9,4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,9,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,9,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{9}}=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,9,4) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-2 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,9,4)+(y-9)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,9,4)+(z-4)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,9,4) \\\\ w-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)+\\frac{1}{6}(y-9)-\\frac{1}{4}(z-4) \\\\ w-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{1}{4}z-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{1}{4}z-w+1 & =0 \\\\ 12(\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{1}{4}z-w+1) & =12\\cdot 0 \\\\ 6x+2y-3z-12w+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a247dce-1016-5a74-8aa3-baeeb529100f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c645d1ad-7209-5343-8f48-3586d833b4f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,0,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403726} {:answer/id #uuid "d2196f73-bb73-51d9-8fdd-442f665c7869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,0,-1)$ and $(1,0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403727} {:answer/id #uuid "af9a2b62-523f-5ba8-b214-6d70686e54b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,0,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403728} {:answer/id #uuid "892681cb-994a-563c-a47e-7b8ed91ea9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,0,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403729} {:answer/id #uuid "0dfb3a1a-3a3a-528f-9c59-2f8a8f352f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,0,-2)$ and $(2,0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403730}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403729} :answer-field/id #uuid "72aa1076-cce3-53b6-97d2-101a723b64a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4dda47ef-07a9-5b66-b0c0-061f88baf1dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-73869" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨-2,4,2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-2,4,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨-2,4,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{y} &=-2 \\\\ cx{e}^{y}+2cy &=4 \\\\ -c &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2{e}^{y}=-2\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $y=0$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n-2x{e}^{0}+2(-2)(0)=4\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-2$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2){e}^{0}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-2,0,-2).$"} :step/id #uuid "4ceff0c8-08e1-5f7b-8d92-4c7255be7291" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539054}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16da06c8-5818-5d83-b103-468e96d457f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538281} {:answer/id #uuid "28af7c9a-9d9e-5376-9c5b-6d7fa5e689d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ and $(1,2,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538317} {:answer/id #uuid "84475504-c2ca-5622-a840-6d61ac41fdbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538534} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9f2d3f-0ff9-53c5-b3cd-a63b7c043f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,2,9)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538607} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7c6f53-ab06-5a01-be5d-aa83ddab04f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,2,9)$ and $(1,1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538317} :answer-field/id #uuid "560ffd1b-5a84-5953-b5a7-cc2f4832a155" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "278532bd-d5b2-5391-ac62-5dd37f8bae10" :question/math-academy-id "q-129553" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-12y \\\\  & =12(2{x}^{2}-y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =-12x+3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3({y}^{2}-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨12(2{x}^{2}-y),\\,3({y}^{2}-4x),\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}12(2{x}^{2}-y)=0 \\\\ 3({y}^{2}-4x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=2{x}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({(2{x}^{2})}^{2}-4x) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{4}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of the last equation are $x=0,1.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({0}^{3})-12(0)(0)+{0}^{3}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our first point is $(0,0,1).$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({1}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({1}^{3})-12(1)(2)+{2}^{3}+1=-7.\n$$\n\nSo, our second point is $(1,2,-7).$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(0,0,1)$ and $(1,2,-7).$"} :step/id #uuid "9423629b-5335-5a54-8c35-42763251427c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5818aebe-14d2-5cfc-9808-7df8a5a99d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2y+4z-w-8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385587} {:answer/id #uuid "ba61275e-1282-5b92-8fbf-13fc0a1de178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-2y+4z-w-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385588} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a3e7eb-d02d-5b58-99ab-47beb608be27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2y-4z+w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385589} {:answer/id #uuid "3817da57-edc7-5c07-a945-5c744c30b6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2y-4z-w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385590} {:answer/id #uuid "b02a4bb9-e8c1-55dd-b5e1-8d443c34fb3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-2y-4z+w+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385590} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c44c80b-fac5-529e-a9a1-b7e072fc7f5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4ac97e9a-8ad0-507c-9c2d-206be970e2b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-73845" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}$ at the point $(1,1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})=-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1) & =2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1) & =2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) & =-4\\cdot 1=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) \\\\ w & =2(x-1)+2(y-1)-4(z-1) \\\\ w & =2x-2+2y-2-4z+4 \\\\ -2x-2y+4z+w & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y-4z-w & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f1913391-3335-5bf3-ba14-e044d24b9294" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539057}}] :activity/title "Tangent Planes to Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538221}]}
